{"findings":[{"path":"pkg/explorer/fleet/admission.go","symbol":"function:*AdmissionService.Outstanding","severity":"medium","claim":"Outstanding and Report are not limited to admission records. Outstanding lists every DispatchClaimed record owned by the same principal, including ordinary dispatch claims held by remote workers, and returns each record's ClaimID as TokenID. Report accepts any claimed record that passes authorizedCurrent and passes the ClaimID to CompleteClaim. Any session of the principal holding invoke can therefore learn a worker's live claim fence and use it to complete that worker's action, including an EffectMutatesExternal action, through the admission report path.","witness":"for _, record := range page.Actions { if record.State != DispatchClaimed { continue } if !sameActionPrincipal(decision, record) { continue } ... TokenID: append([]byte(nil), record.ClaimID...)  — no check that record.AdmittedEffects is non-empty; Report likewise calls dispatch.CompleteClaim without checking the record is an admission","missing_context":"Whether a dispatch ClaimID is meant to be secret from other sessions of the same principal, and whether the existing /fleet/actions/{id}/complete route already lets the same principal complete without knowing the claim ID. If the ID is not secret, severity drops to low (the admission surface still gains authority over non-admission records)."},{"path":"pkg/explorer/fleet/admission.go","symbol":"function:*AdmissionService.Request","severity":"low","claim":"Request reads the caller-chosen action ID from the global store before any principal or object authorization. It then answers ErrAdmissionConflict (HTTP 409) for an ID already used by anyone, and 200 for a fresh one. That gives any invoke-holding caller an existence oracle over other principals' action and admission IDs, which the Report path explicitly works to prevent. If equivalentEnqueue does not compare Subject/Actor/fingerprint, it is worse: a second principal replaying an identical request with the same TokenID is handed the first principal's live grant.","witness":"current, readErr := dispatch.store.GetAction(ctx, request.ID) ... if readErr == nil { return s.replay(...) } → replay: if !equivalentEnqueue(current, base) { return AdmissionGrant{}, ErrAdmissionConflict }; the admissionError mapping sends this to ErrorConflict","missing_context":"The remaining fields compared by equivalentEnqueue (the diff is truncated after Action), and whether the pre-existing dispatch enqueue already exposes the same oracle through ErrActionConflict."},{"path":"pkg/explorer/fleet/admission.go","symbol":"function:*AdmissionService.obligations","severity":"low","claim":"Without a restrictor, per-reference obligations fail open. Each declared reference is authorized against the request's own SourceID/PolicyID, not against the referenced document's registration. A caller with retrieve on the action's scope can therefore declare documents from other sources or compartments and have them admitted unwithheld. NewAdmissionService accepts a nil Restrictor, so any deployment that omits the wiring gets this behaviour silently instead of failing closed.","witness":"decision.AuthorizeObject(auth.OperationRetrieve, auth.ResourceRequest{... SourceID: request.SourceID, PolicyID: request.PolicyID, ObjectID: reference}, now) ... admitted := permitted; if s.restrictor != nil { ... }; and NewAdmissionService only rejects a nil Dispatch","missing_context":"Hosted startup (main.go) wires Restrictor: client, which mitigates this there. It is unknown whether other constructions of AdmissionService, or a nil *authorized.Client, can reach production."}],"unknowns":["Whether DispatchService.Claim can reclaim a claimed record once its lease has expired. If it can, an expired admission (whose egress may already have happened) becomes claimable and executable dispatch work, which is the 'refusal/permission through a different door' the design says it prevents. TestAdmissionNeverLeavesClaimableWork only checks Pull.","Whether the existing dispatch complete/cancel routes can operate on admission records. That would bypass Report's one-shot token semantics, since dispatch completion replays a differing terminal report as recorded.","The full body of equivalentEnqueue beyond the shown fields, specifically whether it compares principal and authorization fingerprint.","How ApplyAction handles a duplicate transitionToken for concurrent first-writes with the same ID. The token covers ID, idempotency key and version, but not TokenID or declaration; the codec comment suggests a DeepEqual check.","Implementations of boundAdmission.requestContext's resolver, dispatch.begin, authorizedCurrent, sameActionPrincipal, resolveCurrentRevisions and restrictCoOccurrence, which were not supplied.","Whether client in openService can be a nil *authorized.Client. Stored in a non-nil interface, it would pass the s.restrictor != nil check."],"summary":"The admission seam is carefully fail-closed in most paths: single-write grant/deny, declaration pinned into retry identity, restrictor intersection, and uniform not-found on Report. The main concrete gap is that Outstanding and Report operate on any claimed dispatch record of the principal, not only admissions. This exposes worker claim IDs and lets the admission report path complete ordinary dispatch actions. Secondary issues are an action-ID existence oracle via the global pre-authorization GetAction in Request, and fail-open per-reference obligations when no restrictor is wired. All findings are proposals that need adjudication against the full source."}
